message:
Interesting! So the HEAD commit is actually the *fix* commit (597b36c), which introduced the bug `len = strncpy(...)`. Wait, let me re-read. The commit message says "Fix missing strncpy in fix_stun_check_message_integrity_str". The diff shows:

Before (parent):
```c
if (ct == TURN_CREDENTIALS_SHORT_TERM)
    strncpy((char *)pwd, (const char *)upwd, sizeof(password_t));
```

After (HEAD, current):
```c
if (ct == TURN_CREDENTIALS_SHORT_TERM) {
    len = strncpy((char *)pwd, (const char *)upwd, sizeof(password_t) - 1);
    pwd[sizeof(password_t) - 1] = 0;
}
```

So the current HEAD has the bug `len = strncpy(...)` which assigns the pointer return value to `len`. This is the vulnerability. The fix would be to not assign `len` from `strncpy`'s return value.

Let me check the parent commit to see what the original code looked like and understand the actual vulnerability better.

<｜｜DSML｜｜=execute_bash>
<parameter=command>cd /workspace/repo-vul/src-vul/coturn && git show 597b36c~1:src/client/ns_turn_msg.c | sed -n '1960,1980p'</｜｜DSML｜｜>

